==Classical Nahuatl==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|[-toːn]|lang=nci}}

===Suffix===
{{head|head=-tōn|nci|suffix}}

# {{context|added to nouns|lang=nci}} A [[diminutive]] suffix; {{non-gloss definition|forms nouns denoting smallness, and rarely insignificance}}.
#: {{term|ahaztli|lang=nci||wing}} → {{term|ahaztōntli||lang=nci|small wing}}
#: {{term|conētl||lang=nci|child}} → {{term|conētōntli||lang=nci|small child}}
#: {{term|māitl|lang=nci||hand}} → {{term|mātōntli||lang=nci|small hand}}

====Usage notes====
* The diminutive element {{term|-tōn|lang=nci}} is inserted between the the noun stem and the absolutive or possessive ending if there is one.
* The suffix {{term|-tōn|lang=nci}} takes the plural form {{term|-tōton|-totōn(tin)|lang=nci}}, undergoing [[reduplication]] plus the addition of the plural suffix {{term|-tin|lang=nci}}.
:: {{term|piltōntli||lang=nci|small child}} → {{term|pīpiltotōntin||lang=nci|small children}}
* If the stem does not take an [[absolutive]] suffix (i.e. ''-li'', ''-tl'' or ''-tli''), the resulting diminutive does not either, and {{term|-tin|lang=nci}} is omitted in the plural.
:: {{term|chichitōn||lang=nci|small dog}} → {{term|chichitotōn||lang=nci|small dogs}}
* The same can also happen with nouns taking the absolutive {{term|-in|lang=nci}}.
:: {{term|quimichin|lang=nci||mouse}} → {{term|quimichtōn|lang=nci||small mouse}}, but {{term|citlālin|lang=nci||star}} → {{term|citlāltōntli|lang=nci||small star}}

====Derived terms====
{{suffixsee|nci}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|diminutive postposition}} {{l|nci|-pīl}}, {{l|nci|-tzin}}

